{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Step 7 sibling sweep — triage of all 77 occurrences\n\n**13 defects, 64 legitimate.** Every hit in `src/rebar/` was read in context rather than\npattern-matched; the full per-site table is in the ticket description's ACs. Summary of the\ndiscrimination:\n\n| bucket | n | rule |\n|---|---|---|\n| DEFECT | 13 | composes a store path bypassing the resolver |\n| LEGITIMATE-DEFAULT | 4 | the default name *inside* a resolver |\n| LEGITIMATE-BOUNDARY | 5 | composes a path that is correctly not the configured store |\n| PROSE | 55 | docstring / comment / error text / argparse help |\n\n**Two named suspects were REFUTED, and refuting them is load-bearing** — mechanically rewriting\nthem would have introduced regressions:\n- `runtime.py:44` (`_DEFAULT_TRACKER_DIR`) is the default inside the reconciler's OWN resolver\n  `_resolve_tracker_dir` (`:330-336`), which already honours an absolute `tracker.dir`.\n- `_snapshot/ticket_view.py:73` and `_snapshot/repo_snapshot.py:586` build paths inside a\n  `mkdtemp`/snapshot directory the code itself just created. Relocation cannot affect them\n  because nothing else ever resolves those paths.\n- `llm/config.py:317` adds a denylist SUPERSET entry beside a real `tracker_dir()` call at `:322`.\n  Denying more paths than necessary is safe.\n- `_commands/fsck.py:253` builds the default name deliberately AS the comparison subject for a\n  \"you changed `tracker.dir` without migrating\" hint.\n\n### The `# tickets-boundary-ok` marker was a rubber stamp\n7 of the 13 defects already carried one. The convention is documented\n(`docs/architecture.md:325-341`) but **no gate ever enforced it**, so it accumulated as\ndecoration. `reconcile.py:452` states the rationale outright — *\"The reconciler resolves the\nstore directly, so use the `.tickets-tracker` boundary here — not config.tracker_dir()\"* — and\nthat claim is false: `ReconcilerSettings.tracker_dir` (`runtime.py:91`, populated at `:290`)\nis exactly such a resolver. The comment is being removed with the fix.\n\n## AC14 gate — `scripts/check_tickets_boundary.py`, wired into `make lint`\n\nAST-based, so comments are excluded structurally rather than by heuristic. It flags a string\nliteral that NAMES the store only in four composing positions — a `/` join, an `os.path.join`\nargument, a `Path(...)` argument, or a name bound to the bare dir name. Measured precision on\nthe current tree: **19 flagged, and 0 of the 55 prose occurrences among them** (12 defect sites\ncarrying the literal + the 7 legitimate ones). The 13th defect,\n`reconcile_helpers.py:234`, holds no literal — it consumes `git_adapter.TRACKER_DIR` — so it is\ncovered by an AC rather than by the gate.\n\nSanction is `# tickets-boundary-ok: <reason>` with the reason **mandatory**, matching the\n`# raw-git-ok: <reason>` vocabulary. A bare marker gets its own distinct diagnostic (\"has NO\nREASON\") rather than being reported as unmarked — otherwise a contributor would be told to add\na marker that is already on the line.\n\n## Caller-dependency sweep — CLEARED, and it constrained the fix\n\nEvery CI workflow either creates `.tickets-tracker` as a worktree at the repo root **without**\nsetting `REBAR_TRACKER_DIR` (so the resolver's default returns the identical path), or — in\n`gerrit-verify.yaml:113,415` — exports `REBAR_TRACKER_DIR=\"$PWD/.tickets-tracker\"`, which the\nresolver returns verbatim as the same path. **The change is therefore behaviour-preserving in\nevery existing deployment**; only a relocated store resolves differently, which is the broken\ncase being fixed.\n\nOne genuine behavioural dependency was found and it shapes the fix:\n`git_adapter.TRACKER_DIR` is exported in `__all__` and **pinned by a characterization test**\n(`tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_git_adapter_characterization.py:35` asserts it equals\n`\".tickets-tracker\"`). So AC5 keeps the constant as the committed-tree label and fixes only its\nconsumer, `reconcile_helpers.py:234`. Rewriting the constant would have been the obvious move\nand would have broken that test.\n\n## RED evidence (oracle held out from the fixer)\n\n`tests/interfaces/facades/test_bridge_status_relocated_store.py` — 5 tests, run pre-fix:\n\n    4 failed, 1 passed\n    FAILED ...reads_env_id_from_a_relocated_store\n    FAILED ...error_never_names_the_unconfigured_repo_root_path\n    FAILED ...resolves_the_last_pass_detail_file_in_a_relocated_store\n    FAILED ...cli_succeeds_against_a_relocated_store\n    PASSED ...still_works_in_the_default_in_tree_layout\n\neach failure naming `<repo>/.tickets-tracker/.env-id`. The passing test is the AC16 control in\nthe default in-tree layout — it is what makes the RED legitimate rather than accidental, since\nit proves the fixture and harness are sound and the failure is specific to relocation."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787859758287064667,"uuid":"6e577e95-18c6-4821-9f1e-aa7c254acf00"}